---
/*
 * Section 1 — the hero. Copy verbatim from the approved build brief.
 *
 * NO CTA HERE, and that is not the /about reasoning repeated. The brief puts the page's
 * two buttons in sections 2 and 5, and section 2 begins immediately below this one. A
 * button here would sit roughly 200px above an identical button, which reads as
 * insistence rather than as an offer — and section 2's whole job is to present the two
 * routes side by side, which only works if neither has been pre-empted.
 *
 * NO ILLUSTRATION. Same treatment as /about's hero: the head carries the band on its own,
 * heading left and subhead right, both running to the container edges. A left-aligned
 * head in a 1240px container leaves a third of the band empty, and empty space on a
 * full-width band reads as something failing to load rather than as air.
 *
 * THE SUBHEAD IS ONE SENTENCE OF TWO CLAUSES and it is long. It is set as written — it
 * is the page's whole argument in miniature (call is fastest, email if you would rather)
 * and splitting it would turn a choice into two instructions.
 */
---

<section class="hero">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="hero__head">
      <p class="eyebrow hero__eyebrow">Contact</p>

      <h1 class="hero__title">Two ways to reach us.</h1>

      <p class="hero__subhead">
        If you want to discuss whether AI is worth using somewhere in your business, book
        the call &mdash; twenty minutes, no cost, and it&rsquo;s the fastest route to a
        useful answer. If you&rsquo;d rather write first, the email address below reaches
        us directly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* White. The ladder for this page runs white → cream → surface → warm → olive: five
   * sections, no two neighbours sharing a colour. */
  .hero {
    background: var(--ark-white);
    /* 96/108, matching /about rather than the three illustrated heroes' 60/76. This one
     * is a headline and a paragraph with no artwork to give it height, and at the shared
     * padding it would be the shortest section on the page. The space is the presence. */
    padding-block: 96px 108px;
  }

  .hero__eyebrow {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 22px;
  }

  /* The measure sits on the element carrying the font-size, never on a wrapper — `ch`
   * resolves against the element's own font-size, so on a wrapper it counts 16px
   * characters and cramps a display heading to a third of its width.
   *
   * 18ch, not /about's 17: "Two ways to reach us." is four short words and wants to break
   * once, after "ways", rather than three times. */
  .hero__title {
    font-size: var(--ark-h1);
    line-height: var(--ark-lh-h1);
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    max-width: 18ch;
  }

  .hero__subhead {
    margin-top: 24px;
    font-size: var(--ark-body-lg);
    line-height: var(--ark-lh-body);
    color: var(--ark-body-ink);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  /*
   * Two columns: heading left, subhead right, ending on the same line. The query is the
   * exact complement of the centring rule in base.css — a portrait tablet centres its
   * heading and must not also be holding two columns.
   *
   * The subhead sits in row 2 ONLY, beside the headline and never beside the eyebrow.
   * Spanning both rows anchors it to the eyebrow instead, which starts the supporting
   * text above the thing it supports. `align-self: end` lands its last line on the
   * headline's last line; the space that remains is at the TOP of the right column,
   * which reads as a deliberate stagger rather than as a column that ran out.
   *
   * The terracotta rule is not decoration — without it the right column starts in
   * mid-air, and it lands flush with the eyebrow's baseline, which is what ties the two
   * columns together. Full reasoning is in /about's hero, where it was worked out.
   */
  @media (min-width: 901px) and (orientation: landscape) {
    .hero__head {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(0, 0.9fr);
      column-gap: 64px;
    }

    .hero__eyebrow {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 1;
    }

    .hero__title {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 2;
    }

    .hero__subhead {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 2;
      align-self: end;
      margin-top: 0;
      padding-top: 26px;
      border-top: 3px solid var(--ark-terracotta);
      max-width: none;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .hero {
      padding-block: 52px 60px;
    }

    .hero__eyebrow {
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }

    .hero__subhead {
      margin-top: 18px;
      max-width: 44ch;
    }
  }

  /* Phone and any portrait screen: the heading BLOCK centres, the text inside it does
   * not. Centred prose is harder to read, which matters more than usual under the
   * plain-language rule — but a left-flush block under a centred headline reads as a
   * misalignment, so the box centres even though its text does not.
   *
   * The auto margin belongs in the SAME query that centres the headline, so the two can
   * never disagree. In a `(max-width: 900px)` block a 1024-wide portrait tablet would
   * centre the headline and leave the subhead flush left beneath it. */
  @media (max-width: 900px), (orientation: portrait) {
    .hero__title,
    .hero__eyebrow {
      text-align: center;
      margin-inline: auto;
    }

    .hero__subhead {
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
